1. College characteristics, majors, and post-graduation earnings
Description: Our first dataset idea is a dataset about U.S. colleges and student outcomes. If we choose this data then we will ultimately be examining how factors such as college selectivity, tuition, type of institution, location, or field of study are associated with students’ earnings after graduation. An example of this would be asking whether attending a more selective or expensive college is associated with higher earnings or comparing earnings and student debt across different majors.
Availability: In terms of the availability of the dataset, the U.S. Department of Education's College Scorecard provides public field-of-study data. Variables include tuition and cost of attendance, admission rates, graduation rates, student demographics, student debt, and post-graduation earnings. We plan to collect the data primarily through the College Scorecard API rather than relying solely on preformatted dataset. We should not need to scrape any external websites meaning all data used will be reliable. We consider the College Scorecard a trustworthy source because it is maintained by the U.S. Department of Education and draws from federal administrative and institutional reporting data. However, we would still investigate missing values, differences in reporting across institutions, and how earnings are measured before conducting our analysis.
Research Question: How are college selectivity, course of attendance, and field of study associated with post-graduation earnings and student debt across U.S. colleges? 
College Scorecard Data | https://collegescorecard.ed.gov/data/api-documentation/ -

2. Citi Bike usage and weather
Description: Our second dataset idea is a dataset of Citi Bike trips in New York City. We could investigate what factors influence bike usage, such as time of day, day of the week, season, rider type, or weather conditions. We could ask how temperature and precipitation affect the number or duration of rides, whether members and casual riders have different usage patterns, or which stations and routes are most popular.
Availability: We would combine Citi Bike trip records with NOAA historical weather observations using the date of each bike trip as the shared key. After aggregating Citi Bike trips by date, each observation could represent a day, with variables such as total rides, average ride duration, rider type, temperature, and precipitation. Combining the datasets would allow us to examine relationships between weather and bike usage that neither dataset could answer independently. Citi Bike publishes its own system trip data, while NOAA is a U.S. government agency responsible for collecting and maintaining weather observations. We would check for missing trips, incomplete station information, and missing or unusual weather observations before analysis.
Research Question: How are daily weather conditions associated with Citi Bike usage patterns in New York City, and does this relationship differ between members and casual riders?
NOAA Climate Data Online | https://www.ncei.noaa.gov/cdo-web/

3. Flight delays and airline performance
Description: Our third dataset idea has U.S. domestic flights and delays. We could investigate what factors are associated with flight delays such as airline, airport, departure time, day of the week, flight distance, or season. For example, we could ask which airlines or airports have the highest probability of delays and whether early morning flights are less likely to be delayed or whether delays become worse as the day progresses.
Availability: We would combine BTS On-Time Performance data with NOAA historical weather data. BTS provides individual flight-level information including scheduled and actual departure times, airline, origin and destination airports, cancellations, and delay information. NOAA provides historical weather observations such as precipitation, temperature, wind, and severe weather conditions. We could match weather observations to flights using airport/location and date and restrict the analysis to a manageable period such as one year. Combining the datasets would allow us to examine whether differences in delays across airports, airlines, and departure times remain after accounting for weather conditions. Both sources are maintained by U.S. government agencies. BTS compiles airline operational data, while NOAA maintains historical weather observations. We would investigate missing flights, cancelled flights, missing weather observations, and differences in reporting across airports before analysis.
Research Question: How are weather conditions, departure time, airline, and airport associated with the likelihood and duration of flight delays in the United States?
BTS Airline On-Time Performance Data  |   https://www.transtats.bts.gov/ONTIME/

4. Is online attention associated with stock-market activity?
Description: We had one more idea regarding a data set that we could work on. This idea focuses on the financial industry and shows the correlation between changes in online attention being associated with stock-market activity. We could include publicly traded companies from retail, technology, finance, entertainment, and fashion. Additionally, the way we approach it is by investigating whether sudden increases in Google searches are associated with higher trading volume or stock price volatility. Additionally, we would try to see whether this relationship differs across industries and if product launches or viral events produce observable changes.
Availability: We could combine weekly Google Trends data with historical stock prices and trading volumes and when we do this each observation would represent one company during the week, with variables such as industry, search interest, closing price, weekly return, trading volume, and volatility. We would need to discuss association rather than claim that search interest causes market changes.
Research Question: Are increases in online search attention associated with changes in trading volume and stock-price volatility, and does this relationship differ across industries?
Google Trends | https://trends.google.com/trends/
